<div class="ephemera-page">

  <section class="ep-hero">
    <div class="ep-kicker">Processing procedure · Staff guide</div>
    <h2>From donation to discovery</h2>
    <p>A practical, repeatable workflow for receiving, identifying, organizing, and recording comics-related ephemera.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>The goal: consistent, lightweight processing</h2>
    <p>Each distinct item receives a unique ID, a brief inventory record, and a physical location. The objective is to make materials identifiable and discoverable while keeping routine processing manageable.</p>
    <div class="ep-callout">
      <strong>Processing target:</strong> Most straightforward items should take approximately two to three minutes. Do not attempt full cataloging, extensive research, or detailed condition reporting as part of routine intake.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Before you begin</h2>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Gather your supplies</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Computer with access to the inventory spreadsheet</li>
          <li>Ephemera ID Generator</li>
          <li>Pencil for labeling sleeves or archival labels</li>
          <li>Appropriate binder sleeves or protective housing</li>
          <li>Donation documentation, if provided</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Understand the basic rules</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>One inventory row per distinct item.</li>
          <li>One unique ID per item.</li>
          <li>Never reuse an ID.</li>
          <li>Never write directly on an item.</li>
          <li>Refer unusual or sensitive materials to the librarian.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>The six-step workflow</h2>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">01</div>
      <h3>Receive and retain documentation</h3>
      <p>Keep any accompanying donor information, correspondence, restrictions, and other documentation together. Record who provided the materials and when they were received, using the available information.</p>
      <p>Do not discard notes about permissions, requested acknowledgments, display preferences, or limitations on use.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">02</div>
      <h3>Sort and identify the materials</h3>
      <p>Group items by their physical format and identify the appropriate standardized item type. Separate obviously damaged, fragile, or questionable materials for librarian review.</p>
      <p>Do not assume that items received together must be stored together or that every piece of paper is a flyer.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">03</div>
      <h3>Generate a unique ID</h3>
      <p>Open the <a href="https://clark.libguides.com/c.php?g=1565192&p=11748151">Ephemera ID Generator</a>. Select the correct type and use the next available collection number.</p>
      <p>Generate the identifier and follow the tool's instructions. Use the complete ID exactly as generated.</p>
      <div class="ep-warning">
        <strong>Important:</strong> The collection uses one running number sequence across all item types. Do not create a separate numbering sequence for stickers, postcards, posters, or other formats. Never reuse an assigned number.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">04</div>
      <h3>Label the housing</h3>
      <p>Write the ID lightly in pencil on the appropriate sleeve or archival label. Position the identifier so that it can be seen without removing the item from its protective housing.</p>
      <p>Do not write, stamp, apply adhesive labels, or otherwise mark the original item.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">05</div>
      <h3>House the item and establish its location</h3>
      <p>Place the item in an appropriate binder pocket or designated flat-file location. Record the final physical location using the standard location format.</p>
      <div class="ep-example">
        <strong>Binder example:</strong> Binder 2, p. 14<br>
        <strong>Flat-file example:</strong> Flat File, Drawer 1
      </div>
      <p>Make sure the item can be retrieved using the location recorded in the spreadsheet.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">06</div>
      <h3>Complete the inventory record</h3>
      <p>Add one row to the inventory spreadsheet for each distinct item. Complete the seven standard fields: ID, Text/title, Type, Creator/org, Keywords, Location, and Donor + date received.</p>
      <p>Transcribe what is visible, use a few practical keywords, and leave unknown information blank rather than guessing.</p>
      <p>See the <a href="https://clark.libguides.com/c.php?g=1565192&p=11748464">Inventory Spreadsheet Guide</a> for field definitions and examples.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Quick quality check</h2>
    <div class="ep-callout">
      Before moving on, confirm:
      <ul>
        <li>The item has the correct standardized type.</li>
        <li>The ID is unique and matches the generator output.</li>
        <li>The ID is on the sleeve or housing, not the item.</li>
        <li>The item is protected and physically housed.</li>
        <li>The spreadsheet contains one complete row for the item.</li>
        <li>The recorded location matches its actual location.</li>
        <li>Donor information and restrictions have been retained.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>When to pause and ask</h2>
    <p>Routine processing should be straightforward. Stop and consult the librarian if you encounter an item that is difficult to classify, damaged, potentially sensitive, subject to restrictions, or unsuitable for ordinary housing.</p>
    <p>See <a href="https://clark.libguides.com/c.php?g=1565192&p=11748467">Questions &amp; Exceptions</a> for additional guidance.</p>
  </section>

